There are two versions of the welcome letter for your club, named
\texttt{Welcome packet.txt} and \texttt{Welcome packet.pdf}.
For the next event, you will have a different pair with a
different base name. Instead of editing every command line each
time, keep the shared piece of text in one place. It is easy to
change a value; the trick is to preserve what the value means,
especially when it is empty or supplied by someone else.

\begin{objectives}
\item Assign, retrieve and remove a shell variable without creating a file.
\item Tell the difference between an unset variable and an assigned empty one.
\item Pass several existing arguments on without destroying their boundaries.
\end{objectives}

\section{Give the repeated part a name}
\label{sec:27-assignment}
\index{shell variables}
\index{parameter expansion}

Start a fresh \texttt{sh} and type the lines below. No files are
needed: printing prospective paths is enough to test the names.
An assignment, \texttt{name=value}, stores text under a name;
the dollar sign means to look that name up later. Use letters,
digits and underscores in ordinary portable variable names,
starting with a letter or underscore. Names are case-sensitive.
Use a lower-case, descriptive name for this temporary exercise
to avoid confusing it with a conventional setting such as
\texttt{PATH}.

\begin{terminal}
packet='Welcome packet'
printf '<%s>\n' "${packet}.txt" "${packet}.pdf"
packet='Volunteer guide'
printf '<%s>\n' "${packet}.txt" "${packet}.pdf"
\end{terminal}

The first output has \texttt{Welcome packet} twice, with the two
extensions. The second has \texttt{Volunteer guide} instead.
The earlier output does not change when you reassign the
variable. And no file gains or loses a name: assigning only
changes what the shell will supply the next time it expands
the variable.

Keep the equals sign next to both the variable name and its
value. In an assignment, \texttt{packet='Welcome packet'} is
one shell word. If you write \texttt{packet = 'Welcome packet'},
the shell instead sees a command named \texttt{packet} with
two arguments. A missing-command error here does not mean the
shell needs an addon for variables. It means the line was
read as a different kind of instruction. Likewise,
\texttt{\$packet} on the left would ask the shell to look up
the old value when you meant to name the place to store a new
one.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-27/assignment.tikz}
\caption{Assignment changes a stored value; expansion retrieves it.}
\label{fig:27-assignment}
\VisualDescription{An assignment arrow points to the variable packet, whose value is Welcome packet. A separate arrow leads from that value to a displayed path with a text extension.}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:27-assignment} separates the two actions. The
variable is not itself a path or a document. Nothing prevents
you assigning a path that does not exist, or later using the same
value as a heading. A clearer name such as \texttt{packet\_stem}
lets you remember why the value is there.

\section{Put a fence around the name}
\label{sec:27-braces}

When the next character cannot be part of a variable name, as
with the dot in \cmd|"$packet.txt"|, the shell stops reading the
name there. But an underscore \emph{can} be part of a name.
The braces in \cmd|"${packet}_notes.txt"| explicitly bound the
lookup. Compare the middle arguments here. Start with an unset
\texttt{packet\_notes} so you know what to expect.

\begin{terminal}
unset packet_notes
printf '<%s>\n' "$packet.txt" "$packet_notes.txt"
printf '<%s>\n' "${packet}_notes.txt"
\end{terminal}

After the reassignment above, expect
\texttt{<Volunteer guide.txt>}, \texttt{<.txt>} and
\texttt{<Volunteer guide\_notes.txt>}. In the second argument
the shell looks for \texttt{packet\_notes}, not for
\texttt{packet} plus a suffix. This typo can produce a
plausible output path rather than an immediate error, because
in the ordinary session an unset variable expands to nothing.
Display the fully assembled path before giving it to an
operation that creates or modifies a file.

\begin{quicktable}{p{1.5in}YY}
\toprule
Spelling & Shell action & Common confusion \\
\midrule
\texttt{packet=letters} & Assign the word letters & Do not insert spaces around the equals sign. \\
\texttt{"\$packet"} & Expand the value as one argument & Do not add a dollar sign on the left of an assignment. \\
\texttt{"\$\{packet\}\_notes"} & Look up packet and append a suffix & Braces mark the name, not the boundary of an entire argument. \\
\texttt{unset packet} & Remove the variable & Does not remove any file or retract earlier command output. \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

The braces identify the parameter, while the double quotes
identify a protected shell word. Both have jobs; neither
replaces the other. \cmd|${packet}_notes.txt| without the quotes
can split a value containing a space, even though the name
of the variable is now unambiguous. Revisit Figure~\ref{fig:26-order}
if it is surprising that a correctly identified value can
still yield several arguments.

\section{A blank can be a deliberate choice}
\label{sec:27-empty}
\index{variables!empty and unset}

A club convention might say: ``Add a distribution remark
unless the editor explicitly leaves it blank.'' In that case,
you need a third possibility besides text and no text: nobody
made a decision yet. An unset variable can represent that
third state. An assigned empty one represents the deliberate
blank. Both ordinarily look the same if you simply print
\cmd|"$remark"|.

\begin{terminal}
unset remark
printf '<%s>\n' "${remark-Draft}" "${remark:-Draft}"
remark=''
printf '<%s>\n' "${remark-Draft}" "${remark:-Draft}"
remark='For volunteers'
printf '<%s>\n' "${remark-Draft}" "${remark:-Draft}"
\end{terminal}

Watch the difference when the assignment leaves an empty
string. Without the colon, the default is used only when
the variable is unset. With the colon, the default is used
when it is unset \emph{or empty}. These spellings supply a
value for that expansion; neither changes the variable.
It is fine to begin by remembering only the case you need.
Choose based on whether the blank should be honored, not
on which punctuation looks more familiar.

\begin{quicktable}{YYY}
\toprule
State of remark & \texttt{\$\{remark-Draft\}} & \texttt{\$\{remark:-Draft\}} \\
\midrule
Unset & Draft & Draft \\
Assigned the empty string & Empty string & Draft \\
Assigned For volunteers & For volunteers & For volunteers \\
\bottomrule
\end{quicktable}

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-27/defaults.tikz}
\caption{The two defaulting expressions diverge for an assigned blank.}
\label{fig:27-defaults}
\VisualDescription{From an empty but assigned remark, the no-colon branch preserves an empty string and the colon branch chooses Draft.}
\end{figure}

Figure~\ref{fig:27-defaults} shows where the editor's explicit
blank matters. Suppose you are printing a label with and
without a draft warning. If someone deliberately chose to
remove the warning from the final label, using the colon
would silently restore it. In contrast, if the value were the
name of a required file, an empty name would not be usable
and you might choose to reject it or substitute a documented
default. The two forms give you a choice; neither knows why
the value is blank.

\begin{dialoguebox}{Nothing, on purpose}
\Brian{If both print nothing, why keep an empty variable at all?}
\Stephen{Sometimes nothing means nobody picked a value, so we might supply a default.}
\Brian{That's exactly my objection. I picked a blank line. Why should a default undo it?}
\Stephen{It shouldn't, given that rule. We need to distinguish an assigned blank from a missing assignment.}
\Brian{And if the blank is an unusable filename rather than a line of text?}
\Stephen{Then the rule can change. The shell offers both tests; only the person designing the task knows which state is meaningful.}
\DialogueConclusion{A chosen blank is not a missing choice.}
\end{dialoguebox}

\section{Keep a group of arguments intact}
\label{sec:27-position}
\index{positional parameters}
\index{set@\texttt{set}}

The parameters numbered \texttt{1}, \texttt{2}, and so on
are the shell's positional parameters. A script gets these
from the arguments used to invoke it, after the script's own
name. To experiment without writing a script, \cmd|set --|
replaces the list with whatever follows that marker. Use the
fresh \texttt{sh} session, because this changes that shell's
existing list. The marker ends option processing for
\texttt{set}; a following argument beginning with a hyphen
would still go in the list.

\begin{terminal}
set -- 'Welcome packet.txt' '' 'Volunteer guide.pdf'
printf 'Count: %s\n' "$#"
printf 'First: <%s>\n' "$1"
printf 'Again: <%s>\n' "$@"
\end{terminal}

\cmd|$#| gives the count, so the first line of output is
\texttt{Count: 3}. \cmd|"$1"| expands to the first argument
only, and \cmd|"$@"| gives one argument per position, including
the empty one. The last display therefore puts \texttt{Again:}
before each of three bracket pairs, the middle one empty.

\begin{figure}[htbp]
\centering
\input{figures/chapter-27/forward.tikz}
\caption{Quoted at-sign expansion preserves the argument slots.}
\label{fig:27-forward}
\VisualDescription{Three input slots labeled first, empty and third feed three corresponding output slots. The empty slot is retained, not skipped.}
\end{figure}

Contrast \cmd|"$*"|, which generally joins the values into
one argument with a separator (a space under the default
settings). It can be useful for a display where the original
boundaries are intentionally discarded. It is wrong for
passing a group of names to another command unchanged. You
could not reliably recover the empty argument or the spaces
in the original names from the joined display. Compare both
ways safely using a final marker so that even an empty list
is distinguishable:

\begin{terminal}
printf '<%s>\n' "$*" end
printf '<%s>\n' "$@" end
set --
printf '<%s>\n' "$@" end
\end{terminal}

The first run gives a line with both filenames inside a
single pair, then \texttt{<end>}; the second gives three
separate argument lines before \texttt{<end>}; the final run
gives only \texttt{<end>}. The clearing line supplies zero
positional arguments. Include \cmd|"$@"| as a whole word when
you want this behavior, not as a fragment of a larger word.
Chapter~\ref{ch:30} uses it to iterate through a group's
documents. For position ten and higher, write braces around
the whole number: \cmd|"${10}"|. Without them, \cmd|"$10"|
looks up position one and appends a zero.

\section{Keep the choices separate}
\label{sec:27-workflow}

Think of a small convention for the club folders. All packet
files share a stem. The supplied suffix distinguishes a
notes file from a print file. The optional credit is the
editor's choice. It is possible to jam all of that into one
variable and split it later, but doing so discards the very
distinctions you are trying to preserve. Start the prospective
display with two variables and one literal suffix instead:

\begin{terminal}
packet='June packet'
unset credit
printf 'File: <%s>\nCredit: <%s>\n' "${packet}_print.pdf" "${credit-Committee}"
credit=''
printf 'File: <%s>\nCredit: <%s>\n' "${packet}_print.pdf" "${credit-Committee}"
\end{terminal}

The format now has two \texttt{\%s} slots, in order. Both
runs name the same file. Only the credit changes, from
\texttt{Committee} to empty. To change the print label
for both you would change the common format, not the
values. This preview is a quick way to catch a
logical mistake: if changing a credit also changes the
pathname, revisit which variable supplies the path.

A script can also get values from its caller. The first
positional parameter does not require the caller to know a
particular variable name or modify their shell. Suppose an
optional remark is supplied as the first argument. Set the
positional list once without the remark, once with an
explicitly empty remark, and once with a nonempty value:

\begin{terminal}
set --
printf 'Remark: <%s>\n' "${1-Rehearsal}"
set -- ''
printf 'Remark: <%s>\n' "${1-Rehearsal}"
set -- 'Bring a pencil'
printf 'Remark: <%s>\n' "${1-Rehearsal}"
set --
\end{terminal}

The outputs contain \texttt{Rehearsal}, blank and
\texttt{Bring a pencil}, in that order. The syntax
you used for a named parameter also works for a
numbered one. If the task instead requires one actual
path, count and validate the inputs rather than trying
to put a fake path in the empty slot. The preview
script in Chapter~\ref{ch:30} uses exactly this kind of
count, then checks each supplied value.

When deciding whether to store anything at all, ask where
it will next be used. If the name appears once and is not
expected to vary, a literal quoted pathname can be shorter
and clearer. If three lines depend on the same part and the
part changes together, a well-named variable avoids three
separate edits. If there are already several arguments with
meaningful boundaries, retain the argument list rather
than joining values into one variable. None of the three
choices is the universal rule. The aim is to make the
relationship between the values and their uses visible
in the command.

A forgotten assignment does not necessarily yield an
obvious shell error: quotation can turn the empty
expansion into one empty argument. That is useful
when the blank is intentional and dangerous when a task
requires a value. Before a write, compare the displayed
path with your intention and check the working directory.
Confirming that the shell produced one argument is a
necessary check, not a guarantee that it is the intended
argument.

\section{Name a setting for the reader who comes after you}
\label{sec:27-names}

Naming a setting well is practical, not ceremonial.
\texttt{packet} worked in a few lines because the only
thing in view was the packet. If you write a longer
recipe with two similar names, \texttt{source\_stem} and
\texttt{print\_stem} explain which one goes where.
Keep the values at the point where they are decided.
For example, if the notes and print copy always share a
stem, reference a single \texttt{packet\_stem} from both
commands rather than storing two copies of the same
piece of text. If the reviewer needs an independently
named file, then keep a separately named value.

Direction also matters. A report may tell you which
file would be used, but seeing the word \texttt{Draft}
does not tell you whether the user entered it or your
default supplied it. To test the difference, choose
fixtures where the outcomes differ in a visible way.
Use \texttt{Committee} as the default, then explicitly
assign \texttt{Anonymous} as the alternate. This sounds
like an extra test only until you need to change the
default for the next meeting: it protects a deliberate
override from accidentally shifting with everyone else.

\begin{terminal}
unset credit
display=${credit-Committee}
printf 'Proposed: <%s>\n' "$display"
credit=Anonymous
display=${credit-Committee}
printf 'Proposed: <%s>\n' "$display"
\end{terminal}

The first shows \texttt{Committee}; the second shows
\texttt{Anonymous}. The policy is part of the expansion
that assigns \texttt{display}. It does not assign a
default to \texttt{credit}. If you later change
\texttt{credit} without repeating the assignment to
\texttt{display}, the previous display value remains.
A variable stores the result, not an instruction to
recalculate every time you refer to it. That explains
why commands printed before a reassignment remain
unchanged, and why storing a path for later use can
be a good way to keep a verified spelling.

\section{Give an unknown argument a boundary, not a new meaning}
\label{sec:27-unknown}

Try three values as if someone else had supplied them:
\texttt{\$HOME}, \texttt{*}, and the empty string.
These are not recommended file names; the point is to
learn what happens to characters that look like shell
instructions while they are stored in the argument list.
The first command supplies each literally. The second
passes each as a separate argument to the probe.

\begin{terminal}
set -- '$HOME' '*' ''
printf 'Supplied: <%s>\n' "$@"
set --
\end{terminal}

Expect one printed line for each supplied argument.
The dollar characters do not cause a home lookup on
the way out. The star does not enumerate the current
directory. The empty string is not discarded. The
initial quote marks were syntax; the later quoted at-sign
expansion preserves what they established. The printing
operation uses a fixed format too, so even a percent sign
in supplied text would remain data rather than rewriting
the output format. This is a design worth keeping when
you move from quick experiments to the real script.

One tempting shortcut is to use the quoted star form for
a summary, then read that same joined string as if it
still contained individually recoverable arguments. A
summary for a bulletin may deliberately render ``Welcome
packet and Volunteer guide'' with no further use. But
a command accepting two file paths cannot deduce the
original boundaries from that phrase. There is no
disagreement between the human and the shell: the list
was replaced with a sentence. If both are needed, keep
the argument list and produce the sentence as a
separate display, not as the new source of inputs.

Finally, keep in mind who owns a value. The current shell
owns the variables and practice positional parameters
you set here. Exiting it restores the earlier prompt
without carrying these changes back. A value belongs in
an invocation's arguments if you want the task runner
to choose it each time. An environment setting can give
several tools a shared convention, as Chapter~\ref{ch:28}
explains, but ordinary shell variables do not automatically
gain that wider visibility merely because you write their
names in uppercase.

\section{Exercises and cleanup}

\begin{exercise}{Build a suffix without changing the value}
\label{ex:27-suffix}
Set \texttt{stem} to \texttt{April packet}, then print
\texttt{April packet\_print.pdf} and the unchanged stem.
Do not create either file.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
stem='April packet'
printf '<%s>\n' "${stem}_print.pdf" "$stem"
unset stem
\end{terminal}
Expect two lines, the first with the full suffix and the
second still \texttt{<April packet>}. The last line removes
the practice variable.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Honor the editor's blank}
\label{ex:27-default}
Set \texttt{credit} to an empty string. Show that your
chosen default \texttt{Anonymous} applies when the variable
is unset but not when it is explicitly empty.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
credit=''
printf '<%s>\n' "${credit-Anonymous}"
unset credit
printf '<%s>\n' "${credit-Anonymous}"
\end{terminal}
Expect \texttt{<>} then \texttt{<Anonymous>}. The unset on the
third line supplies the second test and also cleans up the
variable.
\end{solution}

\begin{exercise}{Forward a short list}
\label{ex:27-forward}
Put \texttt{-first}, an empty argument, and
\texttt{Last name} in the positional parameters. Print the
count, then the individual values. Keep the initial hyphen.
\end{exercise}
\begin{solution}
\begin{terminal}
set -- '-first' '' 'Last name'
printf '%s\n' "$#"
printf '<%s>\n' "$@"
set --
\end{terminal}
Expect \texttt{3}, then \texttt{<-first>}, \texttt{<>}, and
\texttt{<Last name>}. The last line restores an empty list
in the practice shell.
\end{solution}

Exit the fresh \texttt{sh} to discard the remaining shell
variables. There were no files to delete. What other
programs get to see those values while the shell is running
is the subject of Chapter~\ref{ch:28}.

\begin{takeaways}
\item Assign with an unspaced equals sign, expand with a dollar sign, and use braces to mark the name when appending a suffix.
\item Decide whether a blank should be honored before choosing a defaulting expression.
\item Use \texttt{"\$@"} as a whole word to preserve the existing argument boundaries.
\end{takeaways}

